Integer coefficients for the element routines in this addon.


Base functions (shape functions)
--------------------------------

See file addons/README_basefunctions

Numerical integration
---------------------

See file addons/README_Gauss

Procedure pointers
------------------

Summary of assignment of user functions/subroutines to procedure pointers:

  coefficients%func => func
  coefficients%vfunc => vfunc

  coefficients%func1(1)%p => alphafunc   ! scalar function for alpha
  coefficients%func1(2)%p => betafunc    ! scalar function for beta
  coefficients%func1(3)%p => gammafunc   ! scalar function for gamma
  coefficients%func1(4)%p => cbar_func   ! scalar function for embedded bc
  coefficients%func1(5)%p => ibar_func   ! scalar function for embedded interf.
  coefficients%func1(6)%p => jbar_func   ! scalar function for embedded interf.
  coefficients%vfunc1(1)%p => velocityvfunc   ! vector function for velocity
  coefficients%tfunc1(1)%p => alphatfunc      ! tensor function for alpha

  coefficients%set_ninti_user => set_ninti_user
  coefficients%set_Gauss_integration_user => set_Gauss_integration_user

NOTES:

 1) The user functions/subroutines only need to be assigned if they are
    actually being used. If a required assignment is forgotten an error message
    with be given.
 2) The user is free to choose the functions/subroutines for assignment as
    long as the interface is correct.


List of the coefficients
------------------------

1   interpolation (base function)
    for a line
      2: P1
      6: P2
     13: Pp spectral
     19: Pp high order
    for a triangle
      2: P1
      6: P2 
     19: Pp high order
    for a quadrilateral
      4: Q1 
      8: Q2
     13: Qp spectral
     20: Qp high order
    for a hexahedron
      4: Q1 
      8: Q2
    for a tetrahedron
      2: P1
      6: P2 
    for a prism
     15: P1Q1
     16: P2Q2
    for a pyramid
     17: Q1P1r
     18: Q2P2r
    NOTE: for spectral/high-order interpolation also the polynomial order
          needs to be set with coefficients%i(39).

2   The coefficient alpha is as follows:
    0: constant, given by coefficients%r(1) (scalar) or
       coefficients%r(9:17) (tensor)
    1: given by a function, funcnr=coefficient%i(3) (=tfuncnr for a tensor)
       NOTE: scalar function must be assigned to coefficients%func1(1)%p
       NOTE: tensor function must be assigned to coefficients%tfunc1(1)%p
    2: given by nodal values in the vector oldvectors%v(1)
    3: given by Gauss values in the vector per element oldvectors%e(1)

3   funcnr for the coefficient alpha (=tfuncnr for a tensor)

4   Numerical integration rule and number of subdomains for the 
    internal domain integrals of elements:
    0: the same for all elements as given by coefficients%i(10) and 
       coefficients%i(32)
    1: given by the vector per element oldvectors%e(1)
    2: given by user subroutines

5   The coefficient sigma (Robin bc) is as follows:
    0: constant, given by coefficients%r(2)
    Only needed if a matrix build is requested.

6   The normal flux h_N (Neumann boundary) of h_R (Robin boundary)
    is as follows:
    0: constant, given by coefficients%r(23)  (see NOTE2 below).
    1: given by a function, funcnr=coefficient%i(15).
       NOTE: function func must be assigned to coefficients%func
    2: given by a vector function, vfuncnr=coefficient%i(16). The normal flux
       will be computed from the dot product of the specified vector and the
       outside normal vector. The direction of the outside normal is given by
       direction=coefficients%i(17).
       NOTE: function vfunc must be assigned to coefficients%vfunc
    NOTE2: for reasons of compatibility with older versions the value of
    coefficients%i(6) is ignored and assumed to be 1 if
    funcnr=coefficient%i(15)>0 and 2 if vfuncnr=coefficient%i(16)>0.

7-9 Reserved for future use. Set to zero for future compatibility.

10  numerical integration rule (intrule) for the internal domain integrals
    of elements.

11  numerical integration rule (intrule) for boundary integrals for curves,
    surfaces, volumes. For example with intrule=3 (and coefficients%i(40)=0) a
    three-point Gauss-Legendre integration is used for curves and a 3x3
    Gauss-Legendre integration is used for surfaces consisting of quads.

12  funcnr for the right-hand side
    <=0: no right-hand side
     >0: function number in the call to func
    NOTE: see also coefficients%i(18)
    NOTE: function func must be assigned to coefficients%func

13  component choice parameter in computing a derivative.
    See the sources for the possibilities in various derivative routines.

14  Reserved for future use. Set to zero for future compatibility.

15  funcnr for the normal flux h_N (Neumann boundary) of h_R (Robin boundary).

16  vfuncnr for the normal flux vector (Neumann or Robin boundary). 

17  the direction of the outside normal vector (for vfuncnr=coefficients%i(16)
    or for an open boundary) or the normal vector directing outside of the
    contributing part of an interface:
     0: default: the same as giving 1
    -1: opposite to the direction given by the curve or surface
     1: as given by the curve or surface

18  The right-hand side f is as follows:
    0: no right-hand side
    1: given by a function, funcnr=coefficient%i(19)
       NOTE: function func must be assigned to coefficients%func
    2: given by nodal values in the vector oldvectors%v(2)
    3: given by Gauss values in the vector per element oldvectors%e(2)
    NOTE: if coefficient%i(12) > 0, coefficient%i(18) is ignored.

19  funcnr for the right-hand side

20  secondary numerical integration rule (intrule2) for the internal domain
    integrals of elements. This is only relevant for prisms and pyramids.

21  secondary numerical integration rule (intrule2) for boundary integrals.
    This is only relevant for volumes consisting of prisms or pyramids.

22  Reserved for future use. Set to zero for future compatibility.

23  coordinate system for 2D elements:
    0: Cartesian planar
    1: Axisymmetrical
    Note that for axisymmetrical coordinates we have (z,r), i.e. the first
    coordinate is the axial coordinate z and the second coordinate is the
    radial coordinate r.

24  The vector coefficient u (=velocity) is as follows:
    0: constant, given by coefficients%r(4:6)
    1: given by a vector function, vfuncnr=coefficient%i(25)
       NOTE: vector function must be assigned to coefficients%vfunc1(1)%p
    2: given by nodal values in the vector oldvectors%v(3)
    3: given by Gauss values in the vector per element oldvectors%e(3)

25  vfuncnr for the coefficient u (=velocity)

26  The coefficient beta is as follows:
    0: constant, given by coefficients%r(7)
    1: given by a function, funcnr=coefficient%i(27)
       NOTE: scalar function must be assigned to coefficients%func1(2)%p
    2: given by nodal values in the vector oldvectors%v(4)
    3: given by Gauss values in the vector per element oldvectors%e(4)

27  funcnr for the coefficient beta

28  The coefficient gamma is as follows:
    0: constant, given by coefficients%r(8)
    1: given by a function, funcnr=coefficient%i(29)
       NOTE: scalar function must be assigned to coefficients%func1(3)%p
    2: given by nodal values in the vector oldvectors%v(5)
    3: given by Gauss values in the vector per element oldvectors%e(5)

29  funcnr for the coefficient gamma

30-31 Reserved for future use. Set to zero for future compatibility.

32  nsubint: number of subdomains for the numerical integration rule for the
    internal domain integrals of elements:
    0: default: the same as giving 1
    1: only one subdomain, which means apply the numerical integration rule
       given by coefficients%i(10) to the complete element domain.
    >1: divide the element into nsubint subdomains (in each coordinate
        direction) and apply the numerical integration rule given by
        coefficients%i(10) to each subdomain.

33  nsubint: number of subdomains for the numerical integration rule for the
    boundary integrals:
    0: default: the same as giving 1
    1: only one subdomain, which means apply the numerical integration rule
       given by coefficients%i(11) to the complete element domain.
    >1: divide the element into nsubint subdomains (in each coordinate
        direction) and apply the numerical integration rule given by
        coefficients%i(11) to each subdomain.

34--37 Reserved for future use. Set to zero for future compatibility.

38  Layer number. Needs to be specified if the elements are going to be used
    (build,derive etc) for a single layer only.
     0: default: all degrees
     >0: layer number.

39  Polynomial order p of the spectral or high-order interpolation.
    
40  Type of integration (inttype):
      0: standard Gauss-Legendre (analytical expressions)
      1: Gauss-Legendre-Lobatto
      2: mapped Gauss (quad->triangle, hexahedron->pyramid).
      3: standard Gauss-Legendre (numerical tables)
    NOTE: this affects all numerical integration of interior and boundary
    integrals.

41  numerical integration rule for embedded boundary integrals using an eltree
    Note: in 2D interfaces consist of line intervals and in 3D of triangles.

42  The scalar cbar (=prescribed scalar in the embedded
    Diriclet boundary condition) is as follows:
    <0 cbar = 0 (i.e. rhs vector = 0, which saves CPU time)
    0: constant, given by coefficients%r(18)
    1: given by a scalar function, funcnr=coefficient%i(43)
       NOTE: function must be assigned to coefficients%func1(4)%p
    2: given by nodal values in the vector oldvectors%v(6)
    3: given by Gauss values in the vector per element oldvectors%e(6)

43  funcnr for cbar (=prescribed scalar for embedded Dirichlet bc)

44  sign of the "transposed" open boundary flux term
    (element scalar_diffusion_open_boundary_transposed_eltree):
    or the "transposed" interface flux term
    (element scalar_diffusion_flux_interface_transposed_eltree):
    0: default (same as 1)
    1: symmetric version (like in the Nitsche method)
   -1: anti-symmetric version (like in the Baumann-Oden method)

45  The scalar ibar (=prescribed scalar jump in the embedded
    interface condition) is as follows:
    <0 ibar = 0 (i.e. rhs vector = 0, which saves CPU time)
    0: constant, given by coefficients%r(21)
    1: given by a scalar function, funcnr=coefficient%i(46)
       NOTE: function must be assigned to coefficients%func1(5)%p
    2: given by nodal values in the vector oldvectors%v(7)
    3: given by Gauss values in the vector per element oldvectors%e(7)
    NOTE: ibar has been defined as positive if the jump from the first
    layer to the second layer is positive. Be careful here, if the "transposed"
    term is used: the value of ibar should have an opposite sign for the
    separate build of the two layers. Alternatively, the factorvec can be
    given an opposite sign for the two separate builds.

46  funcnr for ibar (=prescribed scalar jump in the embedded
    interface condition).

47  The scalar jbar (=prescribed normal flux jump in the embedded
    interface condition) is as follows:
    <0 jbar = 0 (i.e. rhs vector = 0, which saves CPU time)
    0: constant, given by coefficients%r(22)
    1: given by a scalar function, funcnr=coefficient%i(48)
       NOTE: function must be assigned to coefficients%func1(6)%p
    2: given by nodal values in the vector oldvectors%v(8)
    3: given by Gauss values in the vector per element oldvectors%e(8)
    NOTE: jbar has been defined as positive for supplying "energy" etc. to
    the system.

48  funcnr for jbar (=prescribed normal flux jump in the embedded
    interface condition).

49  Specifies which layer is contributing to the interface condition for this 
    call to the system build (clayer):
    0: default: the same as specifying 1
    1: the first layer
    2: the second layer
    NOTE: this is used in scalar_diffusion_flux_interface_eltree,
          scalar_diffusion2_flux_interface_eltree and
          scalar_diffusion_flux_interface_transposed_eltree
          scalar_ugradc_interface_eltree2
          to avoid specifying material properties/functions/coefficients etc.
          on both sides in one routine.
    NOTE: this requires only two layers to be present at the interface, or at
          least specified in the build (not available yet in build_system).

50  Specifies how the integration rules must be set:
    0: default, i.e. direcly with the value in coefficients.
    1: by specifying the minimal polynomial order that the rule must be able
       to integrate exactly.
    Note, that the value of coefficients%i(50) is overruled by setting
    SET_GAUSS_BY_ORDER = .true. in the module limits_m.

51--100 Reserved for future use. Set to zero for future compatibility.

